  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=5\\sqrt[3]{x}+3.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=5\\sqrt[3]{x}+3\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx=5\\sqrt[3]{y}+3.\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5\\sqrt[3]{y}+3 \\\\ 5\\sqrt[3]{y} & =x-3 \\\\ \\sqrt[3]{y} & =\\frac{1}{5}(x-3) \\\\ (\\sqrt[3]{y})^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{5}(x-3))}^{3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{125}(x-3)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{125}(x-3)^{3}.\n$$",
